CONTEXT: In /luxon/src/impl/util.js, `padStart` is used to zero-pad numbers (e.g. years in `toISODate`, which is implemented via `toTechFormat` -> `Formatter.num(n, p)` -> `padStart(n, p)`). The current implementation is:

  export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
    if (input.toString().length < n) {
      return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
    } else {
      return input.toString();
    }
  }

BUG: it pads by slicing the last n characters of the zero-prefixed string, so negative numbers lose their sign and get the zeros in the wrong place: `padStart(-45, 4)` returns `"00-45"` instead of `"-0045"`. Consequently `DateTime.fromObject({ year: -45, month: 5, day: 25, zone: "utc" }).toISODate()` returns `"00-45-05-25"` instead of the ISO 8601 `"-0045-05-25"`. The sign must be kept in front and the digits padded to n.

TASK:
1) BASELINE: run `cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -40` and record the exact list of failing tests (file + name) and totals.
2) BEFORE the edit, write a scratch script that prints the cases in step 4 and run it, so the before/after difference is visible. Run scratch scripts with `cd /luxon && npx babel-node --only '/tmp' /tmp/check6.js`; if that fails, instead create a scratch test file INSIDE /luxon but NOT under test/ (e.g. /luxon/tmpcheck6.test.js), run `cd /luxon && npx jest --roots . tmpcheck6.test.js 2>&1 | tail -60`, and DELETE it afterwards. Never modify or delete existing test files.
3) Apply exactly this replacement (keep 2-space prettier formatting):

  export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
    const isNeg = input < 0;
    let padded;
    if (isNeg) {
      padded = "-" + padStart(-input, n);
    } else {
      const str = input.toString();
      padded = str.length < n ? ("0".repeat(n) + str).slice(-n) : str;
    }
    return padded;
  }

  Change nothing else in the file. Verify formatting with `npx prettier src/impl/util.js > /tmp/p.js && diff /tmp/p.js src/impl/util.js` (then delete /tmp/p.js).
